  "granularity_workers": 8,
  "captions": {
    "fig5c_granularity": "Figure 5c: Granularity is the master dial: with 8 workers the 200 queries run fastest as tasks of 25 queries (10.0 ms, effective serial fraction f = 0.20); one task with all queries leaves the other workers idle (33.5 ms), and tiny tasks drown in overhead - 2-query tasks (46.6 ms) are even slower than 1-query tasks (17.6 ms) because BLAS switches from a matrix-vector to a matrix-matrix product that first packs the whole corpus.",
    "fig5d_dask_timeline": "Figure 5d: Dask's task stream for one sharded search: the 8 shard tasks run side by side on 8 threads (6.7 ms each) and the single merge - the graph's narrow waist - takes 0.26 ms, only 4% of the 7.5 ms wall time, so a flat merge is enough at this scale.",
    "fig5_parallel_scaling": "Figure 5: With pure Python, processes reach 8.5x (Amdahl p=0.95) while threads stay at 1.0x because of the GIL. For the vectorised search, BLAS threads stop at 2.1x (p=0.55) because the top-k selection after the matmul is single-threaded, whereas strategies that split the work into query chunks or shards also parallelise the selection (ProcessPool+shared memory p=0.74) and the fused Numba kernel has no serial part at all (p=0.98)."
  },
